From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Date: Sat, 19 Feb 94 16:29:01 GMT
Subject: [#94-2-180] Animal Rights (was "rBGH-injected...")

vaald@westminster.ac.uk replies:
>Whoever says that "fought-for" rights > "voluntary" rights?
[...]
>But seriously, what value is a right if it is retractible?

So do you claim that defensible rights are useless, since retractible or
voluntarily granted rights are useless, and rights which have been fought
for and may be defended are no better? 

A retractible right is more valuable than no right at all, just like a
(very) temporary job is better than no job at all.  For example, I'm
temporarily granting spiders in my garden the right to live, and a couple
of days ago one climbing on the back door benefited greatly from this
right.  If they start to become a nuisance in the summer, with large
numbers invading the house, I'll operate a more aggressive policy.  But
if the spiders had the ability to fight for their rights, or conduct
trades with me in return for rights, they'd be in a far stronger position. 

Any ethical system is derived from nothing more than the personal
aesthetic prejudices of its proponents.  "Rights" are not inherent in the
fabric of the universe, they are simply social constructs.  We grant
others rights so that they will reciprocate -- if everyone goes around
shooting each other the loss suffered by the loser is greater than any
advantage gained by the "winner", so it's to everyone's advantage if
everyone observes the right not to be shot at.  Similarly, if someone's
home is burgled, the loss (psychological and material) to the burgled is
greater than the sum the burglar receives from the "fence" for the stolen
goods, so the right not to be burgled is also a useful social construct.
Ultimately people respect rights either to benefit from a mutual trade,
or to avoid being shot at. 

In the case of animals such as cows and squirrels, they are incapable
of reason (apart from the Squirrel :-)), will not understand the concept
of "rights", and so will not reciprocate them.  So they cannot have the
"full" or defensible rights that humans have, but since most of us would
rather be nice to animals we voluntarily grant them semi-rights.  The
exact nature of these semi-rights boils down to personal preferences.
Like where we draw the line...humans, cows, toads, butterflies, wasps,
trees, viruses...  It's not determined by some natural law.

If animals really were undergoing terrible suffering as part of some
manufacturing process, eventually enough people would feel strongly about
it, lobbyists would inform the public, and sales of the product would be
hit.  The market would speak.  Nowadays, even fragrances which don't have
the claim "no animal testing" meet additional sales resistance.  

But there are no "natural" rights beyond our social constructs.  It has
never been satisfactorily demonstrated that rights have an absolute 
existence, and those who claim that animals have some sort of "natural
rights", for example, are left holding the burden of proof. 

Steve Whitrow                           sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk

