Subject: [#94-2-187] SCI: Attotech

   Some time ago, I read about "planckeons" - small almost-closed universes,
with tiny bottlenecks - a thing like this looks like an elementary particle
with electric and other charges equal to the [dis]balance of charges of the
contents of the planckeon.
   If you put N of such things with the same internal diameter within
another such planckeon, and so on, you'll have the volume of such system
grow by the factor of N for linear growth in internal diameter - i.e.,
exponentially.
   Provided you can stuff each bubble with computation-capable medium,
this may allow solving NP-complete problems in linear time.
And in any case, this construct would demonstrate computation speeds
(on some problems) higher than *any* constant; it has no speed limit -
this is what I called "infinite effective" speed.
 
   I consider it all just an intellectual game though.
 
I remember reading a critic of global-decline-thru-resource-depletion
theories who was suggesting that we should
   
   " satisfy existing needs with existing resources,
     hypothetical needs with hypothetical resources,
     and fantastic needs with fantastic resources".
 
   There are too many worries that currently known methods may not be
sufficient for our dream needs.   If we can dream the needs, we can dream
the ways...
 
 
