Subject: [#94-2-188] Death To Vermin!

I posted to ExI-UK, reporting on a TV programme I'd seen about Colombian
society's methods of eliminating poverty (and it wasn't by making the
poor richer!).  Someone suggested I post to the main List, so here it is.

Critics of PPL and anarchism would surely point to substantial
exterminations of the underclasses (if the report is to be believed) in
what they'd term a society "bordering on anarchy" as evidence that things
would be far worse under anarcho-capitalism.  Extropians would doubtless
agree that a true market solution would clearly improve things, although
we wouldn't claim to bring about Utopia.  It's an emotive topic!

-------------------------post originally sent to ExI-UK---------------------
Somewhat related to sterilisation of the poor, did anyone see the
programme a few weeks ago on the "social cleansing" that is carried out
in Colombia, eliminating the homeless "disposables"?

The "disposables" partly consist of muggers and shoplifters, but some of
them make an honest living, recycling and selling stuff from other
people's dustbins and rubbish tips.  These recyclers make about 5 pounds
a day.  Some homeless children said that they never steal, as it is
"against God's Word", instead begging for food and clothes from local
shopkeepers.  Others are taught that "there's no shame in stealing" by
gang leaders.

Much of the killing of these disposables is carried out by professional
hit men who are paid $500 per killing.  This could be attributed to a
failure of the monopoly government law forces to do the job they're
supposed to do.  The police claim to be too short of resources to deal
with the social cleansing problem due to the demands placed on them by
drug-related violence.  (It would be interesting to know how things would
work out under a polycentric PPL system -- would any innocent people end
up with no protection, due to poverty?  Outlaws would be at the mercy of
the public.)  

Some killings were the work of the police and university security guards.
The Government carried out a PR exercise, in which the police provided
some disposables with clothes and allowed them to shower.  People were
then offered jobs in return for giving up their guns, but soon afterwards
they started getting shot.  After 3 months 15 out of 200 had been murdered.

In another incident, one of the recyclers was out on his rounds, and a
university security guard invited him to collect some cardboard.  As he
folded it, he was beaten to the ground, then shot in the head.  28 bodies
were later found in the university, some of them cut into pieces.  It
seems that the recyclers were shot for their body parts, the going rate
was 20 pounds per arm, 30 pounds for a liver, etc, and the guards were
paid only 100 pounds per killing compared with the $500 a head that the
hit men were paid by shopkeepers -- the university murders apparently were
done with the full collusion of senior administrators, who presumably
stood to profit from the trade in corpses.  (I think they gave some prices
in pounds & some in $'s, that's what I've got on my notes.)

Going back to the private liquidations, shops keep getting robbed, so the
traders pay for the social cleansing.  One shopkeeper collected $5,000 for
this "private justice".  The total murder rate in Colombia is 25,000 p.a.
compared with 700 in Britain which has twice the population.  It was said
that social cleansing deaths come to "several thousand" per year, and they
also said that 20 death squads were operating, each killing a dozen a
week.  About 50% of total murders.

Comments by people on the social cleansing: "they [the poor] are worse
than animals", "it's like getting rid of mongrels, and every now and then
an Alsatian is killed as well", "we can no longer walk the streets at
night, so cleansing is now necessary".  One of the hit-men, known as "The
Black Hand", said that every homeless person was just a "social parasite"
and "people are sick and tired of being victims".  He used 7 bullets each
time to make it clear that each death was no accident, but a "social
killing".
---------------------------end of forwarded post----------------------------

Just some more points from my notes that I didn't fit in the above:

Some people were killed simply for being dirty or having long hair.  In
an interview with the President Cesar Garviria (sp?), he was asked
something like whether some people thought the disposables deserved to
die and admitted: "Some citizens think that way, and maybe sometimes kill
these people."  During the section where the narrator was talking about
the murder rate, Colombian society was described as "bordering on anarchy". 

