From: freeman@maspar.com (Jay R. Freeman)
Date: Sun, 20 Feb 94 09:15:43 -0800
Subject: [#94-2-190] Animal Rights (was "rBGH-injected...")

Steve Whitrow observes:
 
> In the case of animals such as cows and squirrels, they are incapable
> of reason (apart from the Squirrel :-)),
 
    The Squirrel blushes profusely.
 
>                                          will not understand the concept
> of "rights", and so will not reciprocate them.  So they cannot have the
> "full" or defensible rights that humans have...
 
    Discussions of rights and their instantiation would be more interesting
if there were more species extant with cognitive facilities close to, but
not identical to, those of humans.  Persons who believe in rights based on
reasoned interactions would then have more data on just what levels of
cognition were required to maintain rights of particular levels of
abstraction and complexity.
    Thus for example, humans have valued certain kinds of cooperative
behavior heavily in selecting which domestic animals shall be allowed to
breed, for a long time, and in partial consequence humans can interact in
ways that are in principle startlingly cooperative with animals that are
potentially dangerous and perhaps no more than a few thousand generations
removed from animals that would have been domestically dangerous.  The
dog is thus related to the wolf.  In the case of the dog, much of this
behavior seems by now to be nearly genetically determined, but training,
practice and continued reinforcement seem to be useful, and the behavior
can often be extended to novel circumstances, as when a particular pet in
a particular home is taught that there is some particular thing that it
must not do.
    Teaching a pet to "behave" is not usually thought of as instantiating
mutual rights, but the Squirrel submits that at the operational level it is
hard to say that it is not.  The Squirrel also has no opinion as to the
degree to which Fido is consciously aware of the issues involved in the
"rights" conferred; yet the Squirrel would very much like to know, and does
not consider the prospect of canine consciousness either to be obvious
nonsense or to be permanently beyond the reach of experimental investigation.
    Larger-brained creatures exhibit more complex behavior.  The Squirrel
recalls reading of an instance in which one of the human companions of
Koko (who is a gorilla who uses American sign language) made the following
offer: ~"Koko, if you will clean up your room, I will give you a cookie."~
Koko accomplished the designated task and then approached her companion
with an outstretched hand.  This instance seems to be a convincing example
of a *contract* with a non-human species.  In previous discussions, members
of the Extropians list have often asserted that the ability to make contracts
is essential to the mutual conferring of rights.  The Squirrel wonders how
the presumed limits of Koko's cognition restrict the rights that she may
share with humans.
    More experiments, and more discussions, might ensue if examples of
_Homo_habilis_ or of _Homo_erectus_ were still around to interact with.
Experiments of a different sort may follow when the trade delegation of
Arcturian giant cockroaches arrives next week -- their average I. Q. is
220, which is quite remarkable considering that cultural differences
tend to reduce their performance on the Stanford-Binet test -- and they
are said to be fond of driving hard bargains...

                              --  Jay Freeman, First Extropian Squirrel

