From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Date: Sun, 20 Feb 94 13:18:24 PST
Subject: [#94-2-192] Wormholes and Multi-Alien Empires

I happened to be looking over Mike's Extropy #11 article on Wormholes,
and realized that Table 1, of distance/times, might not be relevant
after the nearest alien entry (100Mlyr in 19yr at 1g).  

Once your wormhole probe reached the alien frontier, it would be
destroyed unless it slowed down to met up with the alien's holes.  At
that point travel would be limited to traveling through alien holes,
unless they first let you send some of your holes through theirs.  

Now you would be very reluctant to let alien holes come to near the
center of your empire, because if they attacked through them, you
could lose most of your territory, even if you quickly closed all your
holes.  You might even be reluctant to let aliens themselves come near
your center, even if they were transmitted as bits and constructed
anew in each "jump".  Who knows what they could know to do with local
raw materials?

You might allow neighboring aliens to travel along the border of your
empire, if they allowed similar travel along their border in return.
But would you allow their neighbors to travel along your border?  The
more neighbors you allow such travel rights, the more risks you take
that one of them might know how to invade through your holes before
you could close them off.  And allowing distant aliens to carry holes
with them magnifies this risk immensely.

And even if you could close your border holes in response to invaders,
they might still move along your border and completely enclose you,
cutting off your empire from the rest of the universe.  Yes, if they
tried to invade inward, you would have a time multiplier advantage, as
Mike described in his article.  But they might have the advantage of
getting info on technological advances from the rest of the universe,
while you would to do it all yourself.  If they just sat there and
demanded tribute, you might be willing to pay a lot, even if you had
retreated to a "basement universe".

To avoid this fate, you might forbid anyone but neighboring aliens to
carry holes through your border holes.  But a universe like this might
take a very long time to travel across, going one empire at a time.  A
deviating set of aliens could gain great advantage from creating a
universe information highway, allowing info to travel more quickly.

The reduced security of these highway aliens would likely mean war (or
the threat of war) would consolidate these highways into fewer larger
scale highway empires threading their way through the universe.
Non-highway empires would be torn between wanting to be near a highway
(meaning having one of your holes near one of theirs), actually near
two to avoid paying monopoly prices, but being wary of invasion from
those directions.

Another approach to avoiding the risk of enclosure would be to find
distant "sister" empires, which you exchange last-resort holes with,
holes to only be used should you become enclosed.  But its not clear
how you could negotiate to get such holes from your empire to theirs;
owners of holes you would have to pass them through would require
*very* tight security, and likely charge a very high price.  And then
then they might know where your escape hatch is.

Anyway, its not clear you can have a universe where you can travel
very far very fast, without at the same time having lots of empires
much bigger than individual alien "empires".

If knowledge reaches a ultimate limits, then travel/communication
would only be for cultural/entertainment interest, and if economies of
scale are reached, then small empires might defend themselves
indefinitely.  But otherwise, wormholes could allow, and may
induce, the largest imaginable scales of empire and social
organization.  

With wormholes, the distance between two points depends mainly on the
topology of holes, and raw spatial distance becomes irrelevant.  So
spatial distance becomes little inhibition on the scope of empire.

