From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Date: Mon, 21 Feb 94 08:15:43 GMT
Subject: [#94-2-195] Wormholes and Multi-Alien Empires

Robin Hanson offers an interesting analysis:

> I happened to be looking over Mike's Extropy #11 article on Wormholes,
> and realized that Table 1, of distance/times, might not be relevant
> after the nearest alien entry (100Mlyr in 19yr at 1g).

In essence I agree with Robin's analysis.  The following comments are
more to update and extend my own thinking on the subject.  I now think
it would be easier for two alien empires to interpenetrate each other,
making pyrrhic offense easier than I had envisioned, but also making it
easier for a civilisation to scatter escape holes far and wide and able
to come back for revenge.  Basement universes also look like more
desirable and defensible positions, due to the ability to disengage from
the external flow of time.

> Once your wormhole probe reached the alien frontier, it would be
> destroyed unless it slowed down to met up with the alien's holes.

The wormhole probe could penetrate the alien empire time zone if the
hole back at the receiving end is time-dilated to keep in step with the
changing alien empire time.  Hole carrying probes can pass right through
each other's empires, provided the two holes co-ordinate their relative
dilation rates to match the time terrain.  The main disadvantage to such
interlopers is that the time dilation increases the probe journey time. 
To travel n lyr towards an alien centre takes >n yrs (and typically >2n
yrs) in local empire time.  Given the large distances that are likely
to separate alien civilisation centres (>100mlyr?) then there is an
strong incentive to negotiate peaceful passage across alien territory. 
This does mean, though, that the boundaries between empires are blurred.

> And even if you could close your border holes in response to invaders,
> they might still move along your border and completely enclose you,
> cutting off your empire from the rest of the universe.

But would probably take a *very* long time.  Long enough for you to
expand in other directions to prevent enclosure.  This makes total
enclosure less likely.

> Yes, if they tried to invade inward, you would have a time multiplier
> advantage, as Mike described in his article.  But they might have the
> advantage of getting info on technological advances from the rest of 
> the universe, while you would to do it all yourself.  If they just sat
> there and demanded tribute, you might be willing to pay a lot, even
> if you had retreated to a "basement universe".

One advantage of retreating to, and fighting from, basement universes,
which I hadn't realised when I wrote the article, is the extra control
over time it gives a culture.  Any length of time can pass in a basement
universe, with respect to the external universe, by controlling the
time-dilation at the entry holes.  This would tend to put all cultures
on a even footing with regard to technological advances.  An enemy that
could immediately evolve generations of new attack technology, tailored
to the attacker's style, would give pause for thought to most attackers. 
If the attackers respond in kind....  probably a standoff, in terms of
territory at the top level.

> Another approach to avoiding the risk of enclosure would be to find
> distant "sister" empires, which you exchange last-resort holes with,
> holes to only be used should you become enclosed.

Perhaps agencies would arise to meet the demand for bolt holes.  For a
fee they hold some holes.  The best security might be to open diplomatic
relations with as many different cultures as possible.  Hope that in an
information rich universe hostile behaviour brings it own penalties via
reputation damage and ostracisation.

> But its not clear how you could negotiate to get such holes from 
> your empire to theirs; owners of holes you would have to pass them
> through would require *very* tight security, and likely charge a very
> high price.  And then they might know where your escape hatch is.

Don't reveal your identity during negotiations.  Pay in cryptocredits. 
There's always the chance of an agency being subverted or reneging, but
lodging with independent services would reduce the enclosure risk to any
desired level.

> Anyway, its not clear you can have a universe where you can travel
> very far very fast, without at the same time having lots of empires
> much bigger than individual alien "empires".

I agree.

